        "content": "When a professor gives you a paragraph of text about factories, diets, farms, or budgets, **never panic and never try to write the whole model at once**. Read the text with a highlighter and answer these **4 exact questions in order**:\n\n#### Step A: What am I deciding? $\\rightarrow$ Decision Variables ($x_1, x_2, \\dots, x_n$)\nLook at the **very last sentence** of the problem (e.g., *\"How many chairs and tables should the factory produce?\"* or *\"How many hectares should be planted with wheat and corn?\"*).\n- Each unknown quantity you control gets a variable name with **exact units**:\n  - Let $x_1 =$ number of Chairs produced per day.\n  - Let $x_2 =$ number of Tables produced per day.\n\n#### Step B: What is my goal? $\\rightarrow$ Objective Function ($Z$)\nFind the sentence that talks about **money, profit, revenue, cost, or time**:\n- If the goal is **Profit, Revenue, Output, or Return** $\\rightarrow$ write **$\\max Z = c_1 x_1 + c_2 x_2 + \\dots$**\n- If the goal is **Cost, Expense, Waste, or Distance** $\\rightarrow$ write **$\\min Z = c_1 x_1 + c_2 x_2 + \\dots$**\n\n#### Step C: What restricts me? $\\rightarrow$ Structural Constraints\nEvery limited resource (wood, machine hours, budget, storage space, minimum contract demand) becomes **one row (one inequality)**.\nTo write a constraint without mistakes, make sure **both sides have the exact same unit**:\n$$(\\text{Resource used per unit of } x_1)\\cdot x_1 + (\\text{Resource used per unit of } x_2)\\cdot x_2 \\;\\; \\le \\text{ or } \\ge \\;\\; (\\text{Total Resource Available})$$\n\n| English / French Phrase in Problem Text | Math Symbol | Meaning |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| *\"At most\"*, *\"Cannot exceed\"*, *\"Available capacity is\"*, *\"Maximum of\"* | $\\le$ | You cannot use more than you have |\n| *\"At least\"*, *\"No less than\"*, *\"Minimum requirement of\"* | $\\ge$ | You must meet or beat this floor |\n| *\"Exactly\"*, *\"Must equal\"*, *\"Full budget must be spent\"* | $=$ | Strict equality (no leftover allowed) |\n\n#### Step D: Can my variables be negative? $\\rightarrow$ Sign Restrictions\nYou cannot manufacture $-4$ chairs or plant $-2$ hectares of wheat! Always write the **non-negativity constraints** at the bottom:\n$$x_1 \\ge 0, \\quad x_2 \\ge 0$$"
