        "title": "Lesson 1.2 — How to Plot Any Constraint Line in 15 Seconds (The Two-Intercept Trick)",
        "content": "Once you have a 2-variable Linear Program, how do you draw an inequality like $3x_1 + 4x_2 \\le 12$ on graph paper?\n\n> **Golden Rule:** A straight line is completely determined by **2 points**. The easiest points to calculate are the **two axis intercepts**—where the line crosses the $x_1$-axis and the $x_2$-axis!\n\n#### Let's do it step-by-step on $3x_1 + 4x_2 \\le 12$:\n1. **Replace the inequality $\\le$ with an equals sign $=$** to get the boundary line equation:\n   $$3x_1 + 4x_2 = 12$$\n2. **Cover $x_1$ with your finger (set $x_1 = 0$) to find the vertical intercept:**\n   $$3(0) + 4x_2 = 12 \\implies 4x_2 = 12 \\implies x_2 = \\frac{12}{4} = 3$$\n   $\\rightarrow$ Plot Point $A = (0, 3)$ on the vertical axis!\n3. **Cover $x_2$ with your finger (set $x_2 = 0$) to find the horizontal intercept:**\n   $$3x_1 + 4(0) = 12 \\implies 3x_1 = 12 \\implies x_1 = \\frac{12}{3} = 4$$\n   $\\rightarrow$ Plot Point $B = (4, 0)$ on the horizontal axis!\n4. **Connect $(0, 3)$ and $(4, 0)$ with a straight ruler.**\n5. **Which side of the line is valid (the half-plane)? Use the $(0,0)$ Origin Test!**\n   Plug $(x_1, x_2) = (0, 0)$ into the inequality $3x_1 + 4x_2 \\le 12$:\n   $$3(0) + 4(0) = 0 \\le 12 \\quad (\\text{TRUE! } ✅)$$\n   Because $(0,0)$ makes the statement **TRUE**, shade (or point arrows) **toward the origin $(0,0)$**!\n   *(If you had $3x_1 + 4x_2 \\ge 12$, then $0 \\ge 12$ would be FALSE ❌, so you would shade **away** from $(0,0)$.)*\n\n#### What if a constraint only has ONE variable, like $x_1 \\le 4$ or $x_2 \\ge 2$?\n- **$x_1 = 4$** is a **vertical wall** passing through $4$ on the horizontal $x_1$-axis. For $x_1 \\le 4$, keep the left side.\n- **$x_2 = 2$** is a **horizontal floor/ceiling** passing through $2$ on the vertical $x_2$-axis. For $x_2 \\ge 2$, keep the upper side."
