        "content": "Suppose we write our Basic Variables ($e_1, e_2$) and Objective ($Z$) in terms of the Non-Basic Variables ($x_1, x_2$). In your professor's course notes (K. ISKAFI), this is called the **Algebraic / Dictionary Form**:\n$$\\begin{aligned}\ne_1 &= 10 - x_1 - x_2 \\\\\ne_2 &= 24 - x_1 - 3x_2 \\\\\nZ &= 0 + 3x_1 + 5x_2\n\\end{aligned}$$\nRight now, $x_1 = 0$ and $x_2 = 0$, so $Z = 0$. Can we increase $Z$? Follow the **3 Golden Rules**:\n\n---\n\n#### Rule 1: Who Enters the Basis? ($x_e$ = Entering Variable / Variable Entrante)\nLook at the objective equation $Z = 0 + 3x_1 + 5x_2$:\n- Increasing $x_1$ by $1$ adds **$+3$** to $Z$.\n- Increasing $x_2$ by $1$ adds **$+5$** to $Z$.\n- **Dantzig's Rule:** Pick the variable with the **largest positive coefficient** in $Z$ (for Maximization). Here $+5 > +3$, so **$x_2$ enters the basis ($x_e = x_2$)**!\n*(And what stays fixed at $0$? The other non-basic variable, $x_1 = 0$, stays locked at $0$ during this step!)*\n\n---\n\n#### Rule 2: Who Leaves the Basis? ($x_s$ = Leaving Variable / Variable Sortante & The Ratio Test)\nNow we keep $x_1 = 0$ and slowly increase $x_2$ ($x_2 = 1, 2, 3, \\dots$). Watch what happens to our basic variables $e_1$ and $e_2$:\n- From Row 1 (with $x_1=0$):\n  $$e_1 = 10 - 1 x_2 \\ge 0 \\implies x_2 \\le \\frac{10}{1} = 10$$\n  *(If $x_2$ goes past $10$, $e_1$ becomes negative, which is illegal!)*\n- From Row 2 (with $x_1=0$):\n  $$e_2 = 24 - 3 x_2 \\ge 0 \\implies 3x_2 \\le 24 \\implies x_2 \\le \\frac{24}{3} = 8$$\n  *(If $x_2$ goes past $8$, $e_2$ becomes negative!)*\n\n**Which wall do we hit FIRST as $x_2$ grows from $0$ upward?**\nWe hit **$x_2 = 8$** first (because $\\min(10, 8) = 8$)! At $x_2 = 8$, **$e_2$ drops to $0$**, so **$e_2$ leaves the basis ($x_s = e_2$)**!\n\n> **The Minimum Ratio Test Formula:**\n> $$\\theta^* = \\min_{i \\text{ with } a_{ie} > 0} \\left\\{ \\frac{b_i}{a_{ie}} \\right\\}$$\n\n---\n\n#### Rule 3: WHY Do We Ignore Zero or Negative Coefficients ($a_{ie} \\le 0$) in the Ratio Test?\nStudents always ask: *\"Why do we ONLY divide when the coefficient of $x_e$ in the constraint row is positive ($a_{ie} > 0$ in $a_{ie} x_e \\le b_i$, or $-a_{ie} x_e$ in the dictionary)?\"*\nImagine a dictionary row said:\n$$e_3 = 5 + 2x_2$$\nAs you increase $x_2$ ($x_2 = 1, 10, 1000, \\dots$), what happens to $e_3$?\n- For $x_2 = 1 \\implies e_3 = 5 + 2(1) = 7 > 0$\n- For $x_2 = 100 \\implies e_3 = 5 + 2(100) = 205 > 0$\n**$e_3$ GROWS instead of shrinking!** It will **never** hit zero and never become negative, no matter how huge $x_2$ gets! That is why a negative coefficient in the tableau (which corresponds to $+2x_2$ in the dictionary) puts **zero limit** on $x_2$ and is skipped ('—') in the ratio test!"
