      "solution": "#### 1. Initial Basic Feasible Solution (Iteration 0)\n- **Non-Basic Variables (v.h.b. set to $0$):** $x_1 = 0,\\; x_2 = 0,\\; x_3 = 0,\\; x_4 = 0$.\n- **Basic Variables (v.b.):** Plugging $x_1=x_2=x_3=x_4=0$ into the 3 equations gives:\n  $$e_1 = 6, \\quad e_2 = 2, \\quad e_3 = 9, \\quad z = 0$$\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Choosing the Entering Variable ($x_e$)\nLook at the objective equation:\n$$z = 3x_1 + 6x_2 + 2x_3 + 4x_4$$\nAll four coefficients ($+3, +6, +2, +4$) are positive, so increasing any of them increases $z$.\nBy Dantzig's rule, we pick the **largest positive coefficient**, which is **$+6$** (in front of $x_2$).\n- Therefore, **$x_2$ enters the basis ($x_e = x_2$)**!\n- The other non-basic variables stay fixed at zero: **$x_1 = x_3 = x_4 = 0$**.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Choosing the Leaving Variable ($x_s$) via the Ratio Test\nSet $x_1 = x_3 = x_4 = 0$ in all three constraint rows and see how much $x_2$ can grow while keeping $e_1, e_2, e_3 \\ge 0$:\n- **Row 1 ($e_1$):**\n  $$e_1 = 6 - 2x_2 \\ge 0 \\implies 2x_2 \\le 6 \\implies x_2 \\le \\frac{6}{2} = 3$$\n- **Row 2 ($e_2$):**\n  $$e_2 = 2 - 1x_2 \\ge 0 \\implies 1x_2 \\le 2 \\implies x_2 \\le \\frac{2}{1} = 2 \\quad \\leftarrow \\text{SMALLEST RATIO!}$$\n- **Row 3 ($e_3$):**\n  $$e_3 = 9 - 3x_2 \\ge 0 \\implies 3x_2 \\le 9 \\implies x_2 \\le \\frac{9}{3} = 3$$\n\nComparing the upper limits on $x_2$:\n$$x_2 = \\min(3,\\; 2,\\; 3) = 2$$\nAs $x_2$ reaches $2$, **$e_2$ hits $0$ first**, so **$e_2$ leaves the basis ($x_s = e_2$)**!\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Performing the Algebraic Substitution (Pivot)\n1. **Isolate the entering variable $x_2$ from the leaving row ($e_2$ row):**\n   $$e_2 = 2 + x_1 - x_2 - 2x_3 - x_4 \\implies \\mathbf{x_2 = 2 + x_1 - e_2 - 2x_3 - x_4}$$\n2. **Substitute this formula for $x_2$ into $e_1$, $e_3$, and $z$:**\n   - **Into $e_1$:**\n     $$\\begin{aligned}\n     e_1 &= 6 - 2x_1 - 2(\\mathbf{2 + x_1 - e_2 - 2x_3 - x_4}) - 5x_3 - 3x_4 \\\\\n     &= 6 - 2x_1 - 4 - 2x_1 + 2e_2 + 4x_3 + 2x_4 - 5x_3 - 3x_4 \\\\\n     &= \\mathbf{2 - 4x_1 + 2e_2 - x_3 - x_4}\n     \\end{aligned}$$\n   - **Into $e_3$:**\n     $$\\begin{aligned}\n     e_3 &= 9 - 2x_1 - 3(\\mathbf{2 + x_1 - e_2 - 2x_3 - x_4}) - 2x_3 - 4x_4 \\\\\n     &= 9 - 2x_1 - 6 - 3x_1 + 3e_2 + 6x_3 + 3x_4 - 2x_3 - 4x_4 \\\\\n     &= \\mathbf{3 - 5x_1 + 3e_2 + 4x_3 - x_4}\n     \\end{aligned}$$\n   - **Into $z$:**\n     $$\\begin{aligned}\n     z &= 3x_1 + 6(\\mathbf{2 + x_1 - e_2 - 2x_3 - x_4}) + 2x_3 + 4x_4 \\\\\n     &= 3x_1 + 12 + 6x_1 - 6e_2 - 12x_3 - 6x_4 + 2x_3 + 4x_4 \\\\\n     &= \\mathbf{12 + 9x_1 - 6e_2 - 10x_3 - 2x_4}\n     \\end{aligned}$$\n\n✅ **New Solution After Iteration 1:** With non-basic variables $x_1 = e_2 = x_3 = x_4 = 0$, the new basic variables are **$x_2 = 2,\\; e_1 = 2,\\; e_3 = 3$** and objective **$z = 12$**! *(Notice $x_1$ still has $+9 > 0$ in $z$, so in a full solve $x_1$ would enter next!)*"
