      "text": "When students first see a Simplex Tableau with rows labeled $c_j$, $z_j$, and $c_j - z_j$, it looks like a wall of random numbers. Let's translate those 3 symbols into plain English using a **Juice Bar story**:\n\nSuppose your Juice Bar is currently making **Mango Smoothies ($x_2$)**, which sell for **\\$5 per cup**.\nNow you are thinking about introducing a brand-new drink: **Strawberry Shake ($x_1$)**. Should you make 1 cup of Strawberry Shake?\n\n1. **What is $c_j$? $\\rightarrow$ \"The Price Tag on the Menu\"**\n   - $c_1 = \\$4$ is simply how much money a customer pays you for **1 cup of Strawberry Shake ($x_1$)**.\n\n2. **What is $z_j$? $\\rightarrow$ \"The Opportunity Cost (What You Lose by Stealing Ingredients)\"**\n   - Your blender and fruit fridge are already 100% busy making Mango Smoothies.\n   - To make **1 cup of Strawberry Shake ($x_1$)**, you have to steal enough fruit and blender time that you are forced to give up **$0.5$ cups of Mango Smoothie** (worth $\\$5 \\times 0.5 = \\$2.50$).\n   - That $\\$2.50$ you **lose** from Mango Smoothies is **$z_1 = \\sum c_{B_i} a_{i1} = \\$2.50$**!\n\n3. **What is $c_j - z_j$? $\\rightarrow$ \"Is the Trade Worth It? (Net Profit Boost per Unit)\"**\n   $$\\underbrace{c_1 - z_1}_{\\text{Net Gain}} = \\underbrace{\\$4.00}_{\\text{Money Gained } (c_1)} - \\underbrace{\\$2.50}_{\\text{Money Lost } (z_1)} = \\mathbf{+\\$1.50 \\text{ per cup!}}$$\n   - If **$c_j - z_j > 0$** (positive!): You gain more than you lose! **Bring $x_j$ into the basis!**\n   - If **$c_j - z_j \\le 0$** (zero or negative) for every single column: Every possible trade loses money or breaks even. **STOP! You have reached the Optimal Tableau!**"
