      "text": "### 1. Why Does $\\le$ Work Automatically, While $\\ge$ and $=$ Fail at $(0,0)$?\n- When every constraint is $\\le$ (like $x_1 + x_2 \\le 10$), we add $+e_1 = 10$. At the origin $(x_1=0, x_2=0)$, we get **$e_1 = +10 \\ge 0$**—which is a valid, legal starting corner!\n- Now suppose a hospital contract says: *\"You must deliver **at least 4 boxes** of medicine ($x_1 + x_2 \\ge 4$).\"*\n  When we subtract a surplus variable $-e_1$, the equation is:\n  $$x_1 + x_2 - e_1 = 4$$\n  What happens if we try to start at $(x_1=0, x_2=0)$?\n  $$0 + 0 - e_1 = 4 \\implies \\mathbf{e_1 = -4 < 0 \\quad (\\text{ILLEGAL! } ❌)}$$\n  Negative variables are forbidden in Simplex! Why did it fail? Because $(0,0)$ violates $x_1 + x_2 \\ge 4$!\n\n### 2. How Does the Big-M Method Fix This? (Temporary Scaffolding $a_i$)\nTo get an instant starting basis $+1$ in that row, we invent a **fake \"Artificial Variable\" $+a_1 \\ge 0$** (think of it as **temporary construction scaffolding**):\n$$x_1 + x_2 - e_1 + a_1 = 4$$\nNow at step 0, we can set $x_1 = x_2 = e_1 = 0$ and start with **$a_1 = 4 \\ge 0$**!\nWait—$a_1$ is fake! In the real world, we **must force $a_1 = 0$** before the algorithm finishes. How do we make sure Simplex kicks $a_1$ out of the basis as fast as possible?\nWe attach a **gigantic nuclear penalty $M$** (where $M = 1,000,000,000$) to $a_1$ in the objective function:\n- For **Maximization ($\\max Z$)**: Subtract **$-M a_1$** (so keeping $a_1 > 0$ destroys your profit!).\n- For **Minimization ($\\min Z$)**: Add **$+M a_1$** (so keeping $a_1 > 0$ makes your cost explode!)."
