        "solution": "#### Part A: Detecting an Unbounded Solution in 1 Pivot\nAdd slacks $e_1, e_2 \\ge 0$ and build **Tableau 0**:\n\n| $c_B$ | Basis | $x_1$ ($3$) | $x_2$ ($2$) | $e_1$ ($0$) | $e_2$ ($0$) | RHS ($b_i$) | Ratio ($b_i / a_{i1}$) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| $0$ | $e_1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | — ($a_{11} = -1 \\le 0$) |\n| $0$ | **$e_2$** $\\leftarrow$ | **$[1]$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | **$1 / 1 = 1$** (Min) |\n| — | **$c_j - z_j$** | **$+3$** $\\uparrow$ | $+2$ | $0$ | $0$ | **$Z = 0$** | — |\n\n- **Pivot 1:** **$x_1$ enters** ($+3$), **$e_2$ leaves** ($1/1 = 1$). Pivot $p = 1$.\n- **Row Ops:**\n  - $R_2' = R_2 = [1, -1, 0, 1 \\mid 1]$\n  - $R_1' = R_1 + 1 R_2' = [0, 0, 1, 1 \\mid 2]$\n  - $(c_j - z_j)' = [3, 2, 0, 0] - 3[1, -1, 0, 1] = [0, +5, 0, -3]$\n\n**Tableau 1 (After Pivot 1):**\n\n| $c_B$ | Basis | $x_1$ ($3$) | $x_2$ ($2$) | $e_1$ ($0$) | $e_2$ ($0$) | RHS ($b_i$) | Ratio ($b_i / a_{i2}$) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| $0$ | $e_1$ | $0$ | **$0$** | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | **— ($a_{12} = 0 \\le 0$)** |\n| $3$ | $x_1$ | $1$ | **$-1$** | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | **— ($a_{22} = -1 \\le 0$)** |\n| — | **$c_j - z_j$** | $0$ | **$+5$** $\\uparrow$ | $0$ | $-3$ | **$Z = 3$** | — |\n\n- Look at Column $x_2$: it has **$c_2 - z_2 = +5 > 0$**, so **$x_2$ wants to enter**.\n- Now look at the entries in the $x_2$ column: **$a_{12} = 0$** and **$a_{22} = -1$**. **Every entry is $\\le 0$!**\n- Why? Because the equations are $e_1 = 2$ and $x_1 = 1 + x_2$. We can increase $x_2 \\to +\\infty$ (along the ray $x_1 = 1 + x_2$) and $x_1, e_1$ stay positive forever while $Z = 3 + 5x_2 \\to +\\infty$!\n✅ **Conclusion for Part A:** **STOP! The problem has an Unbounded Solution ($Z^* = +\\infty$).**\n\n---\n\n#### Part B: How a Ratio Test Tie Creates a Degenerate Tableau\nAdd slacks $e_1, e_2, e_3 \\ge 0$ to Part B and write **Tableau 0**:\n\n| $c_B$ | Basis | $x_1$ ($3$) | $x_2$ ($2$) | $e_1$ ($0$) | $e_2$ ($0$) | $e_3$ ($0$) | RHS ($b_i$) | Ratio ($b_i / a_{i1}$) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| $0$ | **$e_1$** $\\leftarrow$ | **$[2]$** | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | **$6 / 2 = 3$ (TIE!)** |\n| $0$ | $e_2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4 / 1 = 4$ |\n| $0$ | $e_3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | **$3 / 1 = 3$ (TIE!)** |\n| — | **$c_j - z_j$** | **$+3$** $\\uparrow$ | $+2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | **$Z = 0$** | — |\n\n- **$x_1$ enters** ($+3$). In the Ratio Test, Row 1 ($e_1$: $6/2 = 3$) and Row 3 ($e_3$: $3/1 = 3$) **tie for the minimum ratio of $3$**!\n- By **Bland's smallest-index rule**, between $e_1$ and $e_3$, we pick **$e_1$ to leave** (Pivot $p = 2$ in Row 1).\n- Watch what happens to Row 3 ($e_3$) when we update the tableau ($R_1' = \\frac{1}{2} R_1 = [1, 1/2, 1/2, 0, 0 \\mid 3]$, $R_3' = R_3 - 1 R_1'$):\n\n| $c_B$ | Basis | $x_1$ ($3$) | $x_2$ ($2$) | $e_1$ ($0$) | $e_2$ ($0$) | $e_3$ ($0$) | RHS ($b_i$) |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| $3$ | **$x_1$** | $1$ | $1/2$ | $1/2$ | $0$ | $0$ | **$3$** |\n| $0$ | **$e_2$** | $0$ | $1/2$ | $-1/2$ | $1$ | $0$ | **$1$** |\n| $0$ | **$e_3$ (Degenerate!)** | $0$ | $-1/2$ | $-1/2$ | $0$ | $1$ | **$0$** |\n| — | **$c_j - z_j$** | $0$ | **$+1/2$** | $-3/2$ | $0$ | $0$ | **$Z = 9$** |\n\n✅ **Look at Row 3:** The basic variable **$e_3$ equals $0$** in the RHS column! That is a **Degenerate Basic Feasible Solution** (geometrically, the three lines $2x_1+x_2=6$, $x_1=3$, and $x_2=0$ all intersect at the same corner $(3,0)$!)."
