        "title": "Lesson 5.3 — Complementary Slackness (Théorème des Écarts Complémentaires): The Exam #1 Favorite!",
        "content": "In almost every Oran 1 Final Exam (including Exam Exercise 1!) and TD3/TD4, you will see this exact question:\n> *\"Write the Dual problem $(D)$. Given that the Primal optimal solution is $x^* = (\\dots)$, **deduce the Dual optimal solution $y^*$ using Complementary Slackness (sans résoudre le dual)**!\"*\n\n#### First, what is the Economic Intuition of Complementary Slackness?\n1. **Rule A (Resource Slack vs. Shadow Price $y_i^*$):**\n   Suppose at the optimal farm plan, you only used **7 out of 10 hectares of land**, so you have **$e_1^* = 3$ hectares of unused land sitting empty** ($e_1^* > 0$).\n   How much would you pay to rent 1 *more* hectare of land when you already have 3 empty hectares? **\\$0 ($y_1^* = 0$)!**\n   $$\\boxed{\\text{If Primal Constraint } i \\text{ has slack } (e_i^* > 0, \\text{ not tight}) \\implies y_i^* = 0!}$$\n   *(Conversely, if $y_i^* > 0$, then Primal Constraint $i$ must be 100% tight: $e_i^* = 0$!)*\n\n2. **Rule B (Positive Production $x_j^* > 0$ vs. Dual Constraint $j$):**\n   If the factory actually produces product $x_j$ at the optimum (**$x_j^* > 0$**), then the resources used to make 1 unit of $x_j$ must be worth **exactly** its selling price $c_j$ (zero dual slack $\\omega_j^* = 0$):\n   $$\\boxed{\\text{If } x_j^* > 0 \\implies \\text{Dual Constraint } j \\text{ is a strict EQUALITY } (= c_j)!}$$\n   *(Conversely, if Dual Constraint $j$ is a strict inequality $> c_j$, then $x_j^* = 0$!)*\n\n---\n\n#### The Mathematical Formula of Complementary Slackness:\nFor every Primal Constraint $i = 1, \\dots, m$ (with slack $e_i$) and every Dual Constraint $j = 1, \\dots, n$ (with surplus $\\omega_j$):\n$$\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\ne_i^* \\cdot y_i^* = 0 & \\forall i = 1, \\dots, m \\quad \\text{i.e., } \\left(b_i - \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j^*\\right) \\cdot y_i^* = 0 \\\\\nx_j^* \\cdot \\omega_j^* = 0 & \\forall j = 1, \\dots, n \\quad \\text{i.e., } x_j^* \\cdot \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^m a_{ij} y_i^* - c_j\\right) = 0\n\\end{cases}}$$"
