        "title": "Example 5.2 (Oran 1 Final Exam 2025/2026 — Exercise 1): Complementary Slackness Deduction",
        "badge": "Level 2 — Official Oran 1 Final Exam Problem",
        "problem": "Consider the Primal Linear Program $(P)$ from **Oran 1 Final Exam (Exercise 1)**:\n$$\\max Z = 3x_1 + 2x_2$$\n$$\\text{subject to:} \\quad \\begin{cases}\n2x_1 + x_2 \\le 18 & \\text{(Constraint 1 } \\to y_1\\text{)} \\\\\n2x_1 + 3x_2 \\le 42 & \\text{(Constraint 2 } \\to y_2\\text{)} \\\\\n3x_1 + x_2 \\le 24 & \\text{(Constraint 3 } \\to y_3\\text{)} \\\\\nx_1, x_2 \\ge 0\n\\end{cases}$$\nSuppose we already know that the Primal optimal solution is **$x^* = (x_1^*, x_2^*) = (3, 12)$** with **$Z^* = 33$**.\n**Questions:**\n1. Write the Dual problem $(D)$.\n2. **Without solving the Dual by Simplex**, use **Complementary Slackness (les conditions d'écarts complémentaires)** to find the exact optimal Dual solution $y^* = (y_1^*, y_2^*, y_3^*)$ and verify Strong Duality!",
        "solution": "#### Part 1: Writing the Dual Problem $(D)$\nSince $(P)$ has $m = 3$ constraints and $n = 2$ variables, $(D)$ has 3 variables $(y_1, y_2, y_3)$ and 2 constraints:\n$$\\min W = 18y_1 + 42y_2 + 24y_3$$\n$$\\text{subject to:} \\quad \\begin{cases}\n2y_1 + 2y_2 + 3y_3 \\ge 3 & \\text{(Dual Constraint 1, from } x_1\\text{)} \\\\\n1y_1 + 3y_2 + 1y_3 \\ge 2 & \\text{(Dual Constraint 2, from } x_2\\text{)} \\\\\ny_1, y_2, y_3 \\ge 0\n\\end{cases}$$\n\n---\n\n#### Part 2: Deducing $y^* = (y_1^*, y_2^*, y_3^*)$ via Complementary Slackness\nLet's apply the **4-Step Complementary Slackness Recipe** using $x^* = (3, 12)$:\n\n**Step 1: Plug $x^* = (3, 12)$ into all 3 Primal Constraints to check which ones have slack ($e_i^* > 0$):**\n- **Primal Constraint 1:**\n  $$2(3) + 1(12) = 6 + 12 = 18 \\;\\; (= 18 \\implies \\text{Tight! } e_1^* = 0)$$\n- **Primal Constraint 2:**\n  $$2(3) + 3(12) = 6 + 36 = 42 \\;\\; (= 42 \\implies \\text{Tight! } e_2^* = 0)$$\n- **Primal Constraint 3:**\n  $$3(3) + 1(12) = 9 + 12 = \\mathbf{21 < 24} \\;\\; (\\text{NOT tight! Slack } e_3^* = 24 - 21 = 3 > 0!)$$\n\nBy the **first Complementary Slackness condition ($e_i^* \\cdot y_i^* = 0$)**:\n$$e_3^* = 3 > 0 \\implies 3 \\cdot y_3^* = 0 \\implies \\boxed{y_3^* = 0!}$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Check which Primal Decision Variables $x_j^*$ are strictly positive ($>0$):**\nWe are given **$x_1^* = 3 > 0$** and **$x_2^* = 12 > 0$**.\nBy the **second Complementary Slackness condition ($x_j^* \\cdot \\omega_j^* = 0$)**:\n- Since $x_1^* = 3 > 0$, **Dual Constraint 1 must be a strict equality ($= 3$)**:\n  $$2y_1^* + 2y_2^* + 3y_3^* = 3$$\n- Since $x_2^* = 12 > 0$, **Dual Constraint 2 must be a strict equality ($= 2$)**:\n  $$1y_1^* + 3y_2^* + 1y_3^* = 2$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Substitute $y_3^* = 0$ and solve the $2\\times 2$ linear system for $y_1^*$ and $y_2^*$:**\n$$\\begin{cases}\n2y_1^* + 2y_2^* = 3 & \\text{(A)} \\\\\n1y_1^* + 3y_2^* = 2 & \\text{(B)}\n\\end{cases}$$\n- Multiply Equation (B) by $2$:\n  $$2y_1^* + 6y_2^* = 4 \\quad \\text{(B')}$$\n- Subtract Equation (A) from Equation (B'):\n  $$(2y_1^* + 6y_2^*) - (2y_1^* + 2y_2^*) = 4 - 3 \\implies 4y_2^* = 1 \\implies \\boxed{y_2^* = \\frac{1}{4} = 0.25}$$\n- Substitute $y_2^* = \\frac{1}{4}$ into Equation (B):\n  $$y_1^* + 3\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) = 2 \\implies y_1^* = 2 - \\frac{3}{4} = \\boxed{\\frac{5}{4} = 1.25}$$\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Verify Strong Duality ($W^* = Z^*$):**\n$$W^* = 18y_1^* + 42y_2^* + 24y_3^* = 18\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) + 42\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) + 24(0) = \\frac{90 + 42}{4} = \\frac{132}{4} = \\mathbf{33 = Z^*} \\quad ✅$$\n\n✅ **Final Answer:** The optimal Dual solution is **$y^* = \\left(\\frac{5}{4},\\; \\frac{1}{4},\\; 0\\right) = (1.25,\\; 0.25,\\; 0)$** with **$W^* = 33$**."
