        "problem": "Consider the Primal Linear Program $(P)$:\n$$\\max Z = 5x_1 + 4x_2 + 3x_3$$\n$$\\text{subject to:} \\quad \\begin{cases}\n2x_1 + 3x_2 + x_3 \\le 5 & (y_1) \\\\\n4x_1 + x_2 + 2x_3 \\le 11 & (y_2) \\\\\n3x_1 + 4x_2 + 2x_3 \\le 8 & (y_3) \\\\\nx_1, x_2, x_3 \\ge 0\n\\end{cases}$$\nUsing Complementary Slackness, prove that **$x^* = (2, 0, 1)$** is the optimal Primal solution and find the exact optimal Dual solution **$y^* = (y_1^*, y_2^*, y_3^*)$**.",
        "solution": "#### Step 1: Write the Dual Problem $(D)$ (from Example 5.1 Part A)\n$$\\min W = 5y_1 + 11y_2 + 8y_3 \\quad \\text{s.t.} \\quad \\begin{cases}\n2y_1 + 4y_2 + 3y_3 \\ge 5 & (x_1) \\\\\n3y_1 + 1y_2 + 4y_3 \\ge 4 & (x_2) \\\\\n1y_1 + 2y_2 + 2y_3 \\ge 3 & (x_3) \\\\\ny_1, y_2, y_3 \\ge 0\n\\end{cases}$$\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2: Plug $x^* = (2, 0, 1)$ into the 3 Primal Constraints\n- **Constraint 1:** $2(2) + 3(0) + 1(1) = 4 + 0 + 1 = 5 \\;(= 5 \\implies \\text{Tight! } e_1^* = 0)$\n- **Constraint 2:** $4(2) + 1(0) + 2(1) = 8 + 0 + 2 = \\mathbf{10 < 11} \\;(\\text{Slack } e_2^* = 11 - 10 = 1 > 0!)$\n- **Constraint 3:** $3(2) + 4(0) + 2(1) = 6 + 0 + 2 = 8 \\;(= 8 \\implies \\text{Tight! } e_3^* = 0)$\n\nSince $e_2^* = 1 > 0$, Complementary Slackness ($e_2^* \\cdot y_2^* = 0$) immediately gives:\n$$\\boxed{y_2^* = 0!}$$\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3: Check Which $x_j^*$ Are Strictly Positive ($>0$)\nIn $x^* = (2, 0, 1)$:\n- **$x_1^* = 2 > 0 \\implies$ Dual Constraint 1 is an EQUALITY ($= 5$):**\n  $$2y_1^* + 4y_2^* + 3y_3^* = 5$$\n- **$x_3^* = 1 > 0 \\implies$ Dual Constraint 3 is an EQUALITY ($= 3$):**\n  $$1y_1^* + 2y_2^* + 2y_3^* = 3$$\n*(Notice that $x_2^* = 0$, so Dual Constraint 2 does NOT have to be an equality—we will check its inequality $\\ge 4$ at the end!)*\n\n---\n\n#### Step 4: Substitute $y_2^* = 0$ and Solve for $y_1^*, y_3^*$\n$$\\begin{cases}\n2y_1^* + 3y_3^* = 5 & (1) \\\\\n1y_1^* + 2y_3^* = 3 & (2)\n\\end{cases}$$\n- Multiply Equation (2) by $2$: $2y_1^* + 4y_3^* = 6$.\n- Subtract Equation (1): $(2y_1^* + 4y_3^*) - (2y_1^* + 3y_3^*) = 6 - 5 \\implies \\boxed{y_3^* = 1}$.\n- Plug $y_3^* = 1$ into Equation (2): $y_1^* + 2(1) = 3 \\implies \\boxed{y_1^* = 1}$.\n\n---\n\n#### Step 5: Check Dual Feasibility of Constraint 2 & Strong Duality!\n- Does $y^* = (1, 0, 1)$ satisfy Dual Constraint 2 ($3y_1 + y_2 + 4y_3 \\ge 4$)?\n  $$3(1) + 1(0) + 4(1) = 7 \\ge 4 \\quad (\\text{TRUE! } ✅)$$\n- Compare $Z^*$ and $W^*$:\n  $$Z^* = 5(2) + 4(0) + 3(1) = 13, \\qquad W^* = 5(1) + 11(0) + 8(1) = 13 \\quad (Z^* = W^* = 13 \\;✅)$$\n✅ **Conclusion:** Because $x^* = (2,0,1)$ is Primal feasible, $y^* = (1,0,1)$ is Dual feasible, and $Z^* = W^* = 13$, both solutions are **proven optimal**!"
