      "text": "### 1. Why Not Just Round the Simplex Answer to the Nearest Integer?\nSuppose you run an airline factory and Simplex tells you the optimal continuous solution is:\n$$x_1^* = 3.6 \\text{ Jumbo Jets}, \\qquad x_2^* = 1.4 \\text{ Cargo Planes}$$\nYou can't fly $0.6$ of a plane! What if you just round $(3.6, 1.4)$ to $(4, 1)$?\n- **Disaster 1 (Infeasible!):** Building $4$ Jumbo Jets might require $42\\text{ tons}$ of titanium when your warehouse only has $40\\text{ tons}$! Rounding pushed you **outside the fence**!\n- **Disaster 2 (Suboptimal!):** Even if rounding stays inside the fence, the true best integer corner might be $(2, 3)$—far away from $(3.6, 1.4)$!\n\n### 2. How Does Branch-and-Bound (Séparation et Évaluation) Solve This?\nSince there is no integer between $3$ and $4$ (the zone $3 < x_1 < 4$ is a \"dead zone\" for integers), we **chop the problem into two child branches**:\n- **Left Child Branch:** Force **$x_1 \\le 3$**\n- **Right Child Branch:** Force **$x_1 \\ge 4$**\n\nAnd for **0/1 Knapsack problems** (where each item $x_j \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ is either **taken $=1$** or **left behind $=0$**), solving the relaxed LP at any node is as easy as **packing a suitcase by Greedy Efficiency Ratio ($c_j / w_j$)**! You sort items from highest bang-for-your-buck to lowest, pack whole items ($1$) until your bag is almost full, and pack a **fraction** of the next item to use up the last drop of budget!"
