        "content": "In **TD4 (Exercises 2 & 3)** and **Oran 1 Final Exam (Exercise 2 — 8 points!)**, you solve a **0/1 Knapsack problem**:\n$$\\max Z = \\sum_{j=1}^n c_j x_j \\quad \\text{subject to} \\quad \\sum_{j=1}^n w_j x_j \\le W, \\quad x_j \\in \\{0, 1\\}$$\nStudents often ask: *\"How do we calculate the fractional solution at Node 0, Node 1, Node 2 without running a Simplex tableau at every node?!\"*\nHere is the **secret 3-step Greedy Fractional Algorithm** that solves any Knapsack node in 20 seconds:\n\n---\n\n#### Step 1 (Once at the Very Beginning): Sort Items by Efficiency Ratio $r_j = \\frac{c_j}{w_j}$!\nFor each item $j$, divide its profit $c_j$ by its weight/cost $w_j$:\n$$r_j = \\frac{\\text{Profit } c_j}{\\text{Weight } w_j}$$\nRank the items from **#1 (highest ratio)** to **#n (lowest ratio)**. **Never change this order!**\n\n---\n\n#### Step 2 (At Any Node $k$): Subtract the Locked Variables ($x_j = 1$) First!\nAt Node $k$, some variables are **fixed by the branches above** (e.g., $x_1 = 1, x_3 = 0$):\n- Start with your total budget $W$.\n- For every item fixed at **$x_j = 1$**, subtract its weight $w_j$ from the budget and add its profit $c_j$.\n  - If the remaining budget $W_{\\text{rem}} < 0$, stop immediately: **Node $k$ is INFEASIBLE ❌!**\n- For every item fixed at **$x_j = 0$**, cross it out ($x_j = 0$, uses $0$ budget).\n\n---\n\n#### Step 3: Fill the Remaining Budget $W_{\\text{rem}}$ Greedily with the Free Items!\nWalk through the remaining **free** items in your **sorted efficiency order (#1, #2, ...)**:\n- **If the next free item's weight $w_j \\le W_{\\text{rem}}$:** Take the **whole item ($x_j = 1$)**, and subtract $w_j$ from $W_{\\text{rem}}$!\n- **If the next free item's weight $w_j > W_{\\text{rem}}$ (it doesn't fit completely!):** Take the **exact fraction** that fills the remaining budget to zero:\n  $$\\boxed{x_j = \\frac{\\text{Remaining Budget } W_{\\text{rem}}}{\\text{Item Weight } w_j} \\in (0, 1)}$$\n  and set all remaining less-efficient free items to **$0$**!\n- That single fractional variable $x_j$ is the **exact variable you branch on next ($x_j = 1$ vs. $x_j = 0$)**!"
