        "content": "In **TD1 (Exercise 4)** and **Oran 1 Final Exam (Exercise 3 — 5 points!)**, you are given a problem that has $\\max(\\dots)$, $\\min(\\dots)$, or absolute values $|u|$ inside the objective or constraints, and asked:\n> *\"Transform (linearise) this mathematical program into an equivalent **Linear Program (LP)**!\"*\n\nMemorize these **3 Linearisation Recipes**—they are guaranteed free points on the exam:\n\n---\n\n#### Trick 1: Linearising a Minimax Objective: $\\min Z = \\max(A_1,\\; A_2,\\; \\dots,\\; A_k)$\nSuppose you want to **minimize the worst (maximum)** of several linear expressions, e.g.:\n$$\\min Z = \\max(3x_1 - x_2,\\;\\; x_1 + 2x_2 - 5)$$\n- **How to linearise it in 2 lines:**\n  1. Introduce a new continuous ceiling variable **$v \\in \\mathbb{R}$** (or simply call it $z$) set equal to $\\max(A_1, A_2, \\dots)$.\n  2. If $v$ is the maximum of $A_1$ and $A_2$, then **$v$ must be greater than or equal to BOTH $A_1$ and $A_2$**!\n  $$\\boxed{\\min Z = v \\quad \\text{subject to:} \\quad \\begin{cases} v \\ge A_1 \\quad (\\text{i.e., } A_1 - v \\le 0) \\\\ v \\ge A_2 \\quad (\\text{i.e., } A_2 - v \\le 0) \\end{cases}}$$\n*(And symmetrically, for **Maximin $\\max Z = \\min(A_1, A_2)$**, write $\\max Z = v$ subject to **$v \\le A_1$ and $v \\le A_2$**!)*\n\n---\n\n#### Trick 2: Linearising an Absolute Value $|X|$ (Method A — Bounding Variable $t_i$)\nWhat actually *is* $|X|$? It is simply $\\max(X, -X)$!\nSo whenever you see $|X|$ in a **Minimization objective** (with a positive weight $+c|X|$) or in a **$\\le$ constraint** (like $|X| \\le b$ or $|X_1| + |X_2| \\le 6$):\n- **Replace $|X|$ by a new variable $t \\ge 0$** and require $t$ to dominate both $+X$ and $-X$:\n  $$\\boxed{|X| \\le t \\iff \\begin{cases} X \\le t \\\\ -X \\le t \\end{cases} \\iff -t \\le X \\le t, \\quad t \\ge 0}$$\n\n---\n\n#### Trick 3: Linearising an Absolute Value $|X|$ (Method B — Positive/Negative Split $u^+ - u^-$)\nAlternatively, any expression $X$ inside $|X|$ can be written as the difference of two non-negative variables:\n$$\\boxed{\\begin{aligned}\nX &= u^+ - u^- \\quad (\\text{with } u^+ \\ge 0,\\; u^- \\ge 0) \\\\\n|X| &= u^+ + u^-\n\\end{aligned}}$$\nLet's check: if $X = -4$ (and we minimize $u^+ + u^-$), then $u^+ = 0, u^- = 4$, so $X = 0 - 4 = -4$ and $|X| = 0 + 4 = +4$! Both Method A and Method B receive full credit on the Oran 1 exam!"
