        "solution": "#### Node 0 (Root LP Relaxation — Drop Integer Requirement)\nFirst, solve the continuous LP relaxation ($x_1, x_2 \\ge 0$). The two lines $2x_1 + x_2 = 6$ and $2x_1 + 3x_2 = 9$ intersect at:\n- Subtract (1) from (2): $2x_2 = 3 \\implies x_2 = 1.5$.\n- Plug $x_2 = 1.5$ into (1): $2x_1 + 1.5 = 6 \\implies 2x_1 = 4.5 \\implies x_1 = 2.25$.\n- **Node 0 Solution:** $(x_1, x_2) = (2.25,\\; 1.5)$ with **$Z_0 = 3(2.25) + 4(1.5) = 6.75 + 6 = 12.75$** ($UB_0 = \\lfloor 12.75 \\rfloor = 12$).\n- Since both $x_1 = 2.25$ and $x_2 = 1.5$ are fractional, let's branch on **$x_2 = 1.5$** (between $1$ and $2$):\n  - **Node 1:** $x_2 \\le 1$\n  - **Node 2:** $x_2 \\ge 2$\n\n---\n\n#### Node 1 (Branch $x_2 \\le 1$)\nMaximize $Z = 3x_1 + 4x_2$ with $x_2 \\le 1$, $2x_1 + x_2 \\le 6$, $2x_1 + 3x_2 \\le 9$:\n- Since $x_2$ has profit $4$ and $x_1$ has profit $3$, set $x_2 = 1$.\n- Then Constraint (1) gives $2x_1 + 1 \\le 6 \\implies x_1 \\le 2.5$, and Constraint (2) gives $2x_1 + 3(1) \\le 9 \\implies x_1 \\le 3$.\n- The tighter bound is $x_1 = 2.5$.\n- **Node 1 Solution:** $(x_1, x_2) = (2.5,\\; 1)$ with **$Z_1 = 3(2.5) + 4(1) = 11.5$** ($UB_1 = 11$).\n\n---\n\n#### Node 2 (Branch $x_2 \\ge 2$)\nMaximize $Z = 3x_1 + 4x_2$ with $x_2 \\ge 2$ and $2x_1 + 3x_2 \\le 9$:\n- At $x_2 = 2$, Constraint (2) gives $2x_1 + 3(2) \\le 9 \\implies 2x_1 \\le 3 \\implies x_1 = 1.5$ (which also satisfies $2(1.5) + 2 = 5 \\le 6$).\n- **Node 2 Solution:** $(x_1, x_2) = (1.5,\\; 2)$ with **$Z_2 = 3(1.5) + 4(2) = 12.5$** ($UB_2 = 12$).\n\n---\n\n#### Branching from Node 2 (Since $Z_2 = 12.5 > Z_1 = 11.5$, Expand Node 2 First!)\nAt Node 2, $x_1 = 1.5$ is fractional! We branch on **$x_1$** ( while keeping $x_2 \\ge 2$):\n- **Node 3 ($x_2 \\ge 2$ AND $x_1 \\le 1$):**\n  With $x_1 = 1$, Constraint (2) allows $2(1) + 3x_2 \\le 9 \\implies 3x_2 \\le 7 \\implies x_2 = 7/3 \\approx 2.333$.\n  - **Node 3 Solution:** $(x_1, x_2) = (1,\\; 7/3)$ with **$Z_3 = 3(1) + 4(7/3) = 12.33$** ($UB_3 = 12$).\n- **Node 4 ($x_2 \\ge 2$ AND $x_1 \\ge 2$):**\n  Plug $x_1 \\ge 2$ and $x_2 \\ge 2$ into Constraint (2):\n  $$2(2) + 3(2) = 4 + 6 = \\mathbf{10 > 9!} \\quad (\\text{Violates Constraint 2!})$$\n  - **Node 4 Status:** **INFEASIBLE ❌ (Pruned!)**\n\n---\n\n#### Branching from Node 3 ($x_1 \\le 1,\\; x_2 = 2.33$) on $x_2$:\n- **Node 5 ($x_1 \\le 1,\\; x_2 = 2$):**\n  With $x_2 = 2$ and $x_1 = 1$: check constraints $2(1)+2 = 4 \\le 6$ ✅ and $2(1)+3(2) = 8 \\le 9$ ✅.\n  - **Node 5 Solution:** **$(x_1, x_2) = (1, 2) \\in \\mathbb{Z}_+^2$ (ALL INTEGER! 🎉)** with **$Z_5 = 3(1) + 4(2) = 11$**!\n  - Set **Champion Lower Bound $LB = 11$**.\n- **Node 6 ($x_1 \\le 1,\\; x_2 \\ge 3$):**\n  With $x_2 = 3$, Constraint (2) gives $2x_1 + 3(3) \\le 9 \\implies 2x_1 \\le 0 \\implies x_1 = 0$.\n  - **Node 6 Solution:** **$(x_1, x_2) = (0, 3) \\in \\mathbb{Z}_+^2$ (ALL INTEGER! 🎉)** with **$Z_6 = 3(0) + 4(3) = 12$**!\n  - Since $12 > LB (11)$, update **Champion Lower Bound $LB = 12$**!\n\n---\n\n#### Can Node 1 ($UB_1 = 11$) Beat Our Champion $LB = 12$?\nLook back at waiting Node 1: its upper bound is $UB_1 = \\lfloor 11.5 \\rfloor = 11 \\le LB (12)$.\n- **Node 1 is PRUNED BY BOUND ($UB_1 = 11 < 12$) ✂️!** (Note: even if you branched Node 1 with $x_1 \\le 2, x_2 = 1$, you would get $(2, 1)$ with $Z = 10 < 12$, or $(3, 0)$ with $Z = 9 < 12$!)\n\n✅ **Final Optimal Integer Solution:** **$x_1^* = 0,\\; x_2^* = 3$** with **$Z^* = 12$**!"
